From: Nex package maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] samba: handle single-ID user namespaces in credential probe
Source: Nex
Upstream: not submitted; suitable for upstream review

Samba runs its credential API test as the current user. A root-mapped build
sandbox can report UID 0 while mapping no other IDs, so the test cannot switch
to UID 1 even though the target Linux kernel provides the required syscalls.

Probe the UID switch before the full behavioral test. If the kernel reports
that UID 1 is not mapped, retain Samba's existing non-root fallback, which
checks that the candidate syscall does not return ENOSYS. Normal root builds
still run the complete credential-switch test.

diff --git a/source3/lib/util_sec.c b/source3/lib/util_sec.c
index 42c9d51..9529640 100644
--- a/source3/lib/util_sec.c
+++ b/source3/lib/util_sec.c
@@ -558,6 +558,20 @@ int main(void)
 		exit(have_syscall());
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A root-mapped user namespace may not map UID 1. In that case the
+	 * full credential-switch test below cannot run, so use the existing
+	 * syscall-existence fallback that Samba uses for non-root builds.
+	 */
+	errno = 0;
+	if (samba_seteuid(1) != 0 && errno == EINVAL) {
+		fprintf(stderr, "UID 1 is not mapped: checking for ENOSYS\n");
+		exit(have_syscall());
+	}
+	if (samba_seteuid(0) != 0) {
+		exit(1);
+	}
+
 	gain_root_privilege();
 	gain_root_group_privilege();
 	set_effective_gid(1);
